 We will consider a to with the initial $n$-branch $B=(\{1\}, \{2\}, \ldots, \{n \})$.
A perfect $n$-plant with roots $\{r_1, \dots, r_m\}$ can be constructed by recursively appending the set $\{i\}$ to one of its subplants for any integer $1 \le i \le n$ minus the set $\{i \}$ already occuring in its subplants. 
By convention the subplant with no outflowers is considered perfect and stops the recursion. It is clear, by construction, that the branching factor for each branch $B$ of the n-plant is less than n, and may be 1 depending on the choice of pivot set when building the k+1-th branch.
