 Since $f(101!)=101!$, it follows that $f(p)=p!$ for all primes $p$. Let's divide $2020 \cdot 2021$ up into a product of primes:
\begin{align*}
2020 \cdot 2021 &= 2^2 \cdot 5 \cdot 19 \cdot 101 \cdot 2021.
\end{align*}
We know that $f$ is multiplicative, so
\begin{align*}
f(2020 \cdot 2021) &= f(2^2) f(5) f(19) f(101) f(2021) \\
&= (2!)^2 (5!) (19!) (101!) (2021!).
\end{align*}
Since $f(p) = p!$ for all primes $p$, the only restriction on the function is that it is multiplicative. Thus, the number of possible values of $f(2020 \cdot 2021)$ is the number of possible functions $f: \mathbb{N}_{>1} \rightarrow \mathbb{N}_{>1}$ that satisfy the following properties:
\begin{align*}
f(2) &= 2!, \\
f(5) &= 5!, \\
f(19) &= 19!, \\
f(101) &= 101!, \\
f(p) &= p! && \text{for primes } p \ne 5, 19, 101.
\end{align*}
For any of the remaining primes $p$, the function can be either $f(p)=p!$ or $f(p)=p!/5!$. This gives a total of $2^4 = \boxed{16}$ possible functions $f$, so $16$ possible values of $f(2020 \cdot 2021)$.

