 Let the five positive integers in increasing order be $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ $d,$ $e.$ By definition, $b$ is the median.
Because the median is one more than the mode, there must either be two $b$'s for all values of $a,$ $c,$ $d,$ $e,$ or $b$ must be the only mode among those five integers. Because $b$ is one less than the mean, there are $(a+b+c+d+e)/(5)$ integers that are equal to $b\!+1.$ Therefore:
\[\begin{aligned} b+1&=\frac{a+b+c+d+e}{5} \\ 5b+5&=a+b+c+d+e \\ 5b+5&=(a+b)+(b+b)+(b+c)+(b+d+e) \end{aligned}\]
In either case, we have that $b$ is equal to all four combinations of running the difference of the numbers 5 (and maybe 1)depending on how we run them, to the addition of $b$.



If $b$ is the only mode, then we have $a=c=d=e$.
If there are two $b$'s in the set, then we have $(a+b+c+d+e)/5-b=b+1$.
\begin{align*}\frac{a+b+c+d+e}{5}-b&=b+1 \\ \frac{5b+5-5b}{5}&=b+1 \\ \frac{5}{5}&=b+1 \\ 1&=b+1 \\ b&=0 \end{align*}
This is a contradiction because all integers are positive.

Therefore, we have the first case: $a=c=d=e$.
Thus, $b$ is the only mode and we are left with $a=b=c=d=e$.
